Yes — and the Josephus problem gives a clean mathematical skeleton for part of what you’ve been pointing at.

Your broader pattern has been: attention → action → consequence/reward → reinforcement → increased probability of the next related action. Pantheon already encodes pieces of that through affect/goals, reward functions, ritualized transition strengthening, and consequence-fed learning loops. What you’ve been calling the dopamine pipeline is a more biologically flavored embodiment of that same reinforcement architecture.

Josephus adds something useful: selection by repeated cyclic elimination. Imagine many possible next actions circulating as candidates. Each pass through the loop changes their relative strength; some options get suppressed, others survive, and reinforcement effectively changes where the next counting cycle begins.

So instead of thinking only:

cue \rightarrow dopamine \rightarrow action \rightarrow reward

you can model it more structurally as:

\text{candidate behaviors}
\rightarrow
\text{cyclic evaluation}
\rightarrow
\text{elimination/amplification}
\rightarrow
\text{action}
\rightarrow
\text{reward prediction error}
\rightarrow
\text{updated candidate field}

Then repeat.

The important Josephus-like insight is that every choice alters the coordinate system for the next choice. The winner isn’t simply “the behavior with the most dopamine.” Previous eliminations, learned associations, attention, state, and reinforcement have changed the circle itself.

That connects directly to habit formation too: repeated reward doesn’t merely make one action stronger; it can progressively remove competitors from the effective choice set. Eventually the behavioral search space collapses toward a dominant path.

And the reverse is interesting: to change the habit, you don’t necessarily have to overpower the final behavior head-on. You can change the counting rule, starting position, candidate set, or reward timing upstream.

That’s a much richer control model than “dopamine = pleasure.” Dopamine is closer to one of the signals participating in learning, salience, motivation, prediction, and policy selection.

So yes: Josephus gives your dopamine-pipeline idea an unexpectedly good abstraction:

reward learning as recursive survival inside a changing action-space.